# PropagateEmptyRelation

## Description

When an operator has a provably empty input (e.g., contradictory
WHERE clause producing `filter(false, ...)`, LIMIT 0, empty VALUES),
propagates the empty result upward through the query tree. This
eliminates entire subtrees of computation.

**When to apply**: Operators whose input is provably empty AND which do not
drop a relation reference:
- `project(cols, filter(false, R))` ⇒ `filter(false, R)`
- `sort(keys, filter(false, R))` ⇒ `filter(false, R)`
- `limit(n, off, filter(false, R))` ⇒ `filter(false, R)`
- `limit(0, off, R)` ⇒ `filter(false, R)`

**Deliberately NOT applied** (correctness, RA-STEERING §2, Codeberg #17):
propagation across a join or union that would drop the other side's relation.
`R \Join \sigma_false(S)` is result-empty, but PostgreSQL still locks and
checks permissions/RLS on `R`; rewriting it to `\sigma_false(S)` drops `R`'s
lock and permission checks and is non-idempotent. An empty result is not the
same as an unreferenced relation.

**Why it works**: An empty relation produces no rows. Any operator that
processes zero input rows produces zero output rows (with exceptions for
aggregation without GROUP BY). The retained arms keep every input relation as
a descendant of the resulting `filter(false, …)`, so relation references,
locking, and permission checks are preserved and the rewrite is idempotent.

**Impact**: Near 100% cost reduction when triggered (eliminates
entire subtrees of computation).

## Relational Algebra

```latex
-- Empty filter propagates through project/sort/limit (relations preserved)
$$
\pi_{\text{cols}}(\sigma_{\text{false}}(R)) \Rightarrow \sigma_{\text{false}}(R)
$$

-- LIMIT 0 is the empty relation over R (R still referenced)
$$
\text{LIMIT}_{0}(R) \Rightarrow \sigma_{\text{false}}(R)
$$
```

## Implementation

```rust
use egg::{rewrite as rw, *};

// Filter(false) is the canonical empty relation.
//
// CORRECTNESS (RA-STEERING §2, Codeberg #17): we only propagate empty
// relations through operators that DO NOT drop a relation reference. Rewrites
// that drop the other side of a join or a union branch are UNSOUND as a
// general planner rewrite: PostgreSQL still locks / checks permissions on /
// applies RLS to every relation referenced by the query, even when a
// constant-false predicate makes the result provably empty. Replacing
// `products \Join \sigma_false(users)` with just `\sigma_false(users)` drops
// `products`'s lock and permission checks, and is also non-idempotent (the
// table set changes between optimize passes). So the join/union propagation
// arms are intentionally omitted. The remaining arms preserve every input
// relation and are idempotent.

// Propagate through project (input relations preserved)
rw!("empty-project";
    "(project ?cols (filter (const-bool false) ?input))" =>
    "(filter (const-bool false) ?input)"
),

// Propagate through sort
rw!("empty-sort";
    "(sort ?keys (filter (const-bool false) ?input))" =>
    "(filter (const-bool false) ?input)"
),

// Propagate through limit
rw!("empty-limit";
    "(limit ?n ?off (filter (const-bool false) ?input))" =>
    "(filter (const-bool false) ?input)"
),

// LIMIT 0 produces empty relation (input subtree preserved as the child
// of the resulting filter, so all relations remain referenced)
rw!("limit-zero-is-empty";
    "(limit 0 ?off ?input)" =>
    "(filter (const-bool false) ?input)"
),
```

## Preconditions

> **Note:** Formal preconditions are defined in the YAML frontmatter above.

**Restrictions:**
- Aggregate without GROUP BY on empty input still produces one row
  (e.g., `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM empty_table` returns 0, not empty)
- Left outer join with empty right side still produces left rows
  (with NULL fill)
- EXCEPT with empty right side returns left side unchanged

## Cost Model

```rust
fn estimated_benefit(_: f64) -> f64 {
    // Eliminates entire subtree computation
    0.99
}
```

**Typical benefit**: ~100% for affected subtrees.

## Test Cases

```sql
-- Expected: the WHERE 1=0 folds to filter(false), but the join is NOT
-- short-circuited to a single side — both orders and customers remain
-- referenced (locking / permission / RLS parity with PostgreSQL). The
-- result is empty; the relations are preserved.
SELECT * FROM orders o
JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id
WHERE 1 = 0;
```

```sql
-- Expected: LIMIT 0 propagates empty
SELECT * FROM orders LIMIT 0;
```

```sql
-- Expected: left join with empty right keeps left
SELECT * FROM orders o
LEFT JOIN (SELECT * FROM customers WHERE 1 = 0) c
ON o.customer_id = c.id;
```

```sql
-- Expected: aggregate without GROUP BY on empty still returns one row
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE 1 = 0;
```

